रोशनी आवाज़ से लगभग दस लाख गुना तेज़ चलती है। बिजली की चमक तुम्हें लगभग फ़ौरन दिख जाती है, पर उसकी गड़गड़ाहट को आने में समय लगता है। हवा में आवाज़ लगभग 340 मीटर प्रति सेकंड चलती है, यानी हर 3 सेकंड में लगभग एक किलोमीटर, या हर 5 सेकंड में एक मील।

चमक और गड़गड़ाहट के बीच के सेकंड गिनो और 3 से भाग दो, किलोमीटर में दूरी मिल जाएगी। यह [[@speed-distance|रफ़्तार और दूरी]] का सीधा-सा गणित है। अगर फ़ासला 30 सेकंड का है, तो तूफ़ान लगभग 10 किलोमीटर दूर है।

{{story:sound-travel}}

The most important part of Matsya-6000 is a sphere just 2.1 metres wide, about the size of a small car's inside. All three crew members sit inside it. At 6,000 metres, the water pushes on every part of the sphere with about 600 times the [[pressure|pressure]] of air at sea level. So the walls are made of titanium [[alloy|alloy]], a strong, light metal, 80 millimetres thick.

Outside the sphere are batteries, thrusters, lights, cameras and robotic arms for collecting rocks and animals. The crew will look out through small, thick windows. A normal dive is planned to last about 12 hours, with enough air and supplies for up to 96 hours in an emergency.

{{story:gaganyaan}}

A pangolin's body is covered with overlapping scales, a bit like the tiles on a roof or the scales on an artichoke. The scales are made of [[keratin|keratin]], the same material as your [[@nails|fingernails]]. They make up about a fifth of the animal's weight, and their edges are sharp enough to cut a curious paw.

When danger comes, the pangolin rolls into a tight ball, tucking its head inside its tail. Lions and leopards have been filmed batting at a curled-up pangolin and finally giving up. The name comes from a Malay word, *pengguling*, meaning "one who rolls up".

{{story:ants-world}}

When a big heap is built well, the [[@microbes-friendly|microbes]] multiply so fast that they make heat, the same way your body warms up when you exercise. The centre can reach 55 to 65 °C within a few days. On a cold morning, you might see steam rising when you open it.

That heat is useful. It kills most weed seeds and harmful germs. Every week or two, gardeners turn the heap with a fork to bring air into the middle and mix the cooler outside into the hot centre. The heap should feel damp, like a sponge that has been squeezed out.

{{story:earthworms}}

In India, much recycling happens through people rather than machines. The kabadiwala visits homes to buy old newspapers, bottles and metal by weight. Lakhs of waste pickers collect and sort materials from streets and dumps, then sell them to dealers who pass them on to factories. Their work keeps huge amounts of material out of landfills.

Many cities, including Pune and Bengaluru, have helped waste pickers form groups, so they can work more safely and earn fairer pay for this hard and often overlooked job.

{{story:ocean-plastic}}

लगभग 3,300 साल पहले मिस्र में नौजवान फ़राओ तूतनख़ामून को ठोस सोने का मुखौटा पहनाकर दफ़नाया गया, जिसमें नीला काँच और लाजवर्द जैसे पत्थर जड़े थे। इसका वज़न लगभग 10 किलो है। मिस्र के लोग मानते थे कि मुखौटा आत्मा को परलोक में अपना शरीर पहचानने में मदद करता है।

1925 में जब [[archaeologist|पुरातत्वविद्]] हावर्ड कार्टर को यह मिला, तो दुनिया की आँखें चौंधिया गईं। आज यह [[@ancient-egypt|प्राचीन मिस्र]] के सबसे मशहूर खज़ानों में से एक है, और लाखों लोग इसे देखने के लिए कतारों में लग चुके हैं।

{{story:art-chhau}}

For most of history, people built with what they could find nearby: wood, stone, mud, [[@bamboo|bamboo]], grass, animal skins or snow. In [[@gujarat-rann|Kutch]], Gujarat, people build round houses called *bhungas*, with thick mud walls and cone-shaped thatched roofs. They stay cool in desert heat, and their round shape helps them resist [[@earthquakes|earthquakes]]. After the 2001 Gujarat earthquake, many bhungas survived when stronger-looking concrete buildings fell.

{{story:earthquakes}}

In Assam, the Mising people build houses on bamboo stilts called *chang ghar* to stay dry when the Brahmaputra floods. In Kerala, old homes have sloping tiled roofs to shed [[monsoon|monsoon]] rain, and some families live on houseboats in the backwaters.

India's first general election, in 1951–52, was one of the biggest experiments in [[democracy|democracy]] ever tried. About 17 crore people could vote, and most of them could not read or write.

Every adult could vote from the very start, men and women alike, rich or poor. In many older democracies, women had waited decades for the vote, as you can read in [[@womens-vote|women's vote]]. To help voters who couldn't read, each party was given a picture symbol. The voting age was 21 until 1989, when it was lowered to 18.

{{story:womens-vote}}

A few years later, Florence Nightingale returned from nursing soldiers in the Crimean War. She had kept careful records and found that far more soldiers died of disease in dirty hospitals than of their wounds. To make busy officials pay attention, she drew a new kind of round chart, now often called a rose diagram. It showed the problem at a glance, and it helped push the army to improve hospital hygiene.

Good [[@graphs-charts|graphs and charts]] are still one of a data scientist's strongest tools. A table of numbers can hide a story. A picture can make it obvious.

{{story:weather-forecasts}}

Bankers use a handy trick. Divide 72 by the yearly interest rate, and you get roughly how many years it takes for money to double. At 6% a year, money doubles in about 12 years. At 8%, about 9 years. At 12%, about 6 years.

It isn't exact, but it's close for normal interest rates. It also works backwards for things that grow in a bad way. If prices rise 6% a year, they double in about 12 years. That's [[@inflation|inflation]], and it eats into savings that don't earn enough interest.

{{story:puzzle-chessboard-rice}}

On 29 September 2010, in the village of Tembhli in Nandurbar district, Maharashtra, a woman named Ranjana Sonawane received the first Aadhaar number. Within about a decade, the great majority of people in India had one.

That makes Aadhaar the largest identity system of its kind in the world. Enrolment is free, and the number itself does not expire. Collecting fingerprints and eye scans from more than 130 crore people meant setting up enrolment centres in post offices, banks and schools in every corner of the country.

{{story:fingerprints}}

In August 1942, during the [[@quit-india|Quit India Movement]], the British arrested Congress leaders and silenced newspapers. A young student in Bombay, Usha Mehta, and a few friends set up a hidden transmitter. Their broadcasts began with the words "This is the Congress Radio calling".

They read out news the government was hiding and messages from underground leaders. To avoid being caught, they kept moving the equipment from building to building. After about three months, police tracked them down and Usha Mehta was jailed. She later became a professor and lived to see India celebrate 50 years of freedom.

{{story:quit-india}}

[[sensor|Sensors]] in the headset track how you move. Tiny devices called gyroscopes and accelerometers, like the ones that rotate your phone screen, feel every tilt and turn. [[@cameras-how|Cameras]] on the headset watch the room to track where you step.

The computer must redraw the world very fast, often around 90 times a second. If the picture lags even slightly behind your head, the world seems to swim, and many people [[@motion-sickness|feel sick]]. That queasy feeling comes from a mismatch: your eyes see movement, but the balance sensors in your [[@ears-hear|inner ear]] don't feel any.

{{story:3d-glasses}}

In September 1838, aged about 20, Douglass borrowed the papers of a free Black sailor and dressed like one. He boarded a train in Baltimore, then a steamboat and more trains. Within a day he reached New York City.

He married Anna Murray, a free Black woman who had helped plan the escape and paid for part of it, and they settled in Massachusetts. To avoid slave catchers, he changed his surname to Douglass.

{{story:speeches-famous}}

The team of about 30 to 40 men left Camp Dubois, near St. Louis, in May 1804. They pushed, rowed and dragged a big keelboat up the Missouri River against the current. Some days they managed only a few kilometres.

They spent their first winter among the Mandan people in what is now North Dakota. There they met [[@usa-sacagawea|Sacagawea]], a young Lemhi Shoshone woman, and her French-Canadian husband, Toussaint Charbonneau. They joined as interpreters. Sacagawea had a baby, Jean Baptiste, in February 1805, and carried him the rest of the way.

{{story:rocky-mountains}}

Students lived in monasteries built around open courtyards, each with small stone cells for sleeping and studying. Days were filled with lessons, debates and prayers.

Debate mattered a lot. Students learned to argue a point and defend it against sharp questions. Nalanda was supported by kings, including Harsha in the 7th century and later the Pala rulers of Bengal. Grants of villages paid for food and supplies, so students did not have to pay fees.

{{story:libraries}}

The Appalachians formed as continents collided while the supercontinent [[@pangaea|Pangaea]] was coming together. When the land that would become Africa pushed into North America, layers of rock crumpled up like a rug pushed against a wall. This is how the [[@himalaya-form|Himalaya]] are being built today, as India pushes into Asia.

Later, Pangaea split. The Atlantic Ocean opened in the middle of the old mountain range and carried pieces away. Some of the same rocks and folds turn up today in Ireland, the Highlands of [[@scotland|Scotland]] and the Anti-Atlas mountains in [[@morocco|Morocco]].

{{story:pangaea}}

J. K. Rowling, a single mother living in Edinburgh, sent her first book to many publishers. About a dozen said no. Bloomsbury in London finally took it. The story often told is that the chairman gave the first chapter to his eight-year-old daughter, who demanded the rest.

*Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone* came out in 1997 with a small first print run. In the USA, it was renamed *Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone*. Word spread mostly from child to child, in playgrounds and classrooms, before newspapers caught on.

{{story:libraries}}

Chaat masala gives chaat its tangy, slightly funky [[@taste-smell-link|taste]]. It usually contains dried mango powder (amchur) for sourness, roasted cumin, black pepper, dried ginger, asafoetida and kala namak.

Kala namak, or black salt, is a rock salt heated with other ingredients. The process creates sulphur compounds, which give it a smell like boiled eggs. A tiny pinch makes a big difference, partly because much of what we call taste is really [[@nose-smell|smell]].

